## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests designing a DP over a scan of the array with extra dimensions to enforce fixed-length, non-overlapping subsequences and reconstructing the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions with two resource dimensions: completed subsequences and in-progress length
- Fixed-length subsequences ensure non-overlap via r reset on completion
- Backtracking reconstructs actual indices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating transitions
for i in 1..n:
  for t in 0..k:
    for r in 0..L-1:
      // skip
      dp[i][t][r] = Math.max(dp[i][t][r], dp[i-1][t][r])
      // take
      if (r==0 && t<=k) {
        dp[i][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][t][1], dp[i-1][t][0] + A[i])
      } else if (r>0 && r<L-1) {
        dp[i][t][r+1] = Math.max(dp[i][t][r+1], dp[i-1][t][r] + A[i])
      } else if (r==L-1) {
        dp[i][t+1][0] = Math.max(dp[i][t+1][0], dp[i-1][t][L-1] + A[i])
      }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize memory to O(kL)?
- How to extend if L varies per subsequence or is not fixed?
- How does negative A affect the choice and reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

